(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_principlesofinheritanceandvariation=[{id:"neet-2026-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sickle-cell anaemia",q:"Which one of the following disorders is caused by the substitution of Glutamic acid (Glu) by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule?",o:["Haemophilia","Thalassemia","Sickle-cell anaemia","Phenylketonuria"],c:2,s:`Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosome-linked recessive trait.
It passes from parents to offspring when both partners are carriers of the gene.
The defect is caused by substitution of Glu by Val at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin.`},{id:"neet-2026-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Inheritance patterns",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Incomplete dominance \u2014 I. Human skin colour
B. Co-dominance \u2014 II. Inheritance of flower colour in Antirrhinum sp.
C. Pleiotropy \u2014 III. Phenylketonuria disease in humans
D. Polygenic inheritance \u2014 IV. ABO blood groups
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],c:0,s:`Flower colour in snapdragon (Antirrhinum sp.) is an example of incomplete dominance, so A-II.
ABO blood groups show co-dominance in individuals of AB group (I\u1D2CI\u1D2E), so B-IV.
Phenylketonuria is pleiotropy, since the gene responsible leads to multiple phenotypic effects, so C-III.
Human skin colour is controlled by three pairs of non-allelic genes, so it is polygenic inheritance, so D-I.`},{id:"neet-2026-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination in honeybees",q:`Which of the following statements are true with reference to the sex-determination in honeybees?
A. An offspring formed from the union of a sperm and an egg, develops as a female (queen or worker).
B. An unfertilized egg develops as a male by parthenogenesis.
C. A male has half the number of chromosomes than that of a female.
D. Males produce sperms by meiosis.
E. Honeybees have a haplodiploid sex-determination system.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B, C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only","A, B, D and E only","A, B, C and E only"],c:3,s:`In honeybees, males have half the number of chromosomes of females.
Females are diploid with 32 chromosomes; males are haploid with 16 chromosomes.
This is the haplodiploid sex-determination system.
A special feature is that males produce sperms by mitosis, not meiosis, so D is false.
True statements: A, B, C and E.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q142",pn:142,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendel's monohybrid cross",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : In an experiment, Mendel observed that the F1 progeny plants are all tall and none are dwarf.
Reason R : Stem height is a contrasting trait, with tall being dominant and dwarf being recessive.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Mendel crossed tall and dwarf plants and found all F\u2081 plants tall.
The tall trait (T) is dominant over the dwarf trait (t).
Because of this dominance, all F\u2081 plants are tall.`},{id:"neet-2025-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Polygenic inheritance",q:"What is the pattern of inheritance for polygenic trait?",o:["Mendelian inheritance pattern","Non-mendelian inheritance pattern","Autosomal dominant pattern","X-linked recessive inheritance pattern"],c:1,s:`Polygenic inheritance means a trait is controlled by two or more genes.
Disorders caused by mutation in a single gene pass on as per Mendelian principle.
A polygenic trait shows a non-Mendelian inheritance pattern.`},{id:"neet-2024-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Test cross",q:"In a plant, black seed color (BB/Bb) is dominant over white seed color (bb). In order to find out the genotype of the black seed plant, with which of the following genotype will you cross it?",o:["Bb","BB/Bb","BB","bb"],c:3,s:`To find the genotype of a black-seeded plant, it is crossed with the white-seeded (homozygous recessive) plant.
This cross is called a test cross.
So the black plant (BB/Bb) must be crossed with bb.`},{id:"neet-2024-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Incomplete dominance",q:"A pink flowered Snapdragon plant was crossed with a red flowered snapdragon plant. What type of phenotype/s is/are expected in the progeny?",o:["Only pink flowered plants","Red, pink as well as white flowered plants","Only red flowered plants","Red flowered as well as pink flowered plants"],c:3,s:`Pink snapdragon has genotype Rr.
Red snapdragon has genotype RR.
Cross Rr x RR gives RR, RR, Rr, Rr.
Phenotype ratio Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.
So only red and pink flowered plants are obtained.`,sfig:{w:380,h:140,alt:"Punnett square of Rr crossed with RR: top R, R; left R, r; cells RR, RR, Rr, Rr; phenotype Red : Pink : White = 2 : 2 : 0.",d:[["r",2,8,234,126],["l",2,134,236,134],["l",2,126,2,134],["l",236,8,236,134],["l",2,8,82,78],["l",82,8,82,134],["l",142,8,142,134],["l",2,78,236,78],["l",2,106,236,106],["t",34,30,"\u2642"],["t",24,66,"\u2640"],["t",92,30,"R"],["t",152,30,"R"],["t",8,96,"R"],["t",92,96,"RR"],["t",152,96,"RR"],["t",8,124,"r"],["t",92,124,"Rr"],["t",152,124,"Rr"],["t",268,28,"Phenotype"],["t",268,96,"Red : Pink : White"],["t",268,122,"2"],["t",296,122,"2"],["t",330,122,"0"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Genetic terms",q:`Mathch List I with List II
List I:
(A) Two or more alternative forms of a gene
(B) Cross of F\u2081 progeny with homozygous recessive parent
(C) Cross of F\u2081 progeny with any of the parents
(D) Number of chromosome sets in plant
List II:
(I) Back cross
(II) Ploidy
(III) Allele
(IV) Test cross
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],c:0,s:`A. Alternative forms of a gene are alleles.
B. F\u2081 crossed with the homozygous recessive parent is a test cross.
C. F\u2081 crossed with either parent is a back cross.
D. The number of chromosome sets in a plant is its ploidy.
So: A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2024-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Law of dominance",q:`Which one of the following can be explained on the basis of Mendel's Law of Dominance?
(A) Out of one pair of factors one is dominant and the other is recessive.
(B) Alleles do not show any expression and both the characters appear as such in F\u2082 generation
(C) Factors occur in pairs in normal diploid plants
(D) The discrete unit controlling a particular character is called factor
(E) The expression of only one of the parental characters is found in a monohybrid cross.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["B, C and D only","A, B, C, D and E","A, B and C only","A, C, D and E only"],c:3,s:`According to the Law of Dominance:
(1) Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.
(2) Factors occur in pairs.
(3) In a dissimilar pair, one factor (dominant) masks the other (recessive).
This law explains why only one parental character is seen in a monohybrid cross.
Statement B (alleles show no expression and both characters reappear in F\u2082) is explained by the Law of Segregation, not dominance.
So A, C, D and E are correct.`}]})();
